low-slice aliasing: lowering splits vector results into unit slices and regathers them with low.concat for wide
consumers (the b128 stores of the two halves of a WMMA accumulator), and coalescing a slice ignored only its source,
not values sharing the source's exact storage (the dead tied accumulator input of the WMMA that produced it), so the
allocator materialized both halves (8 moves per tile in the attention's S exchange). Two parts: low.concat of
consecutive slices of one value canonicalizes to the subrange slice (or the value; not when the value is itself a
concat, whose slices fold into its operands), and slice coalescing also ignores the source's dead exact-storage
aliases, as copy coalescing already did (same liveness check: no use after the clobber point). Attention loop 29 -> 21
moves: fp16 -1.6% at 32K context, kv4 126K-depth chunk attention -2.3%, kv8 32K -0.8%, pp2048 -0.5%; every other
kernel of the reference sets byte-identical; outputs bit-identical. Test expectations updated where the fold or the
removed copy shows (source_low_slice, the fp8 / bf8 MMA conversions, one gfx1250 matrix-window case whose consumer
now reads the accumulator after the same hazard nops instead of a copy of it).

